\subsection{Definitions and mathematical statement}
\paragraph{Shared notation.}Standard number systems are $\mathbb N,\mathbb Z,\mathbb Q,\mathbb R,\mathbb C$. Specialized notation and index ranges follow the cited source and the definitions below.


A quadratically constrained quadratic program has a quadratic objective and quadratic equality or inequality constraints. Numerical conditioning measures how perturbations in the encoded problem affect its solution or residuals; repeated nonlinear operations can amplify errors.
\paragraph{Record scope and qualifications.}
The catalogue title is retained as metadata. Named certificates, contracts, bounds or frameworks in that title are not asserted as results of the original source extract.
The automatic-recognition target has no specified condition threshold, arithmetic model, or algorithmic success criterion.

Source-record limitation: The supplied assessment flags a broad automatic-recognition request without a defined ill-conditioning threshold or target.
\paragraph{Statement recorded in the supplied source.}
Consider a quadratically constrained quadratic program with $$ (x_1-1)^2+x_2^2\geq3+s^2,\qquad a_i-a_{i-1}^2=0,\qquad a_0=s/2,$$ where a finite chain of the $a_i$ amplifies numerical error through repeated square roots. How can solvers automatically recognize such severely ill-conditioned instances? \href{https://doi.org/10.4171/owr/2019/26}{[S2]}
